The file is named `UpdateCampfireExportDateParams.ts`, but its exported type is declared as `UpdateCampfireExporterParams` — the basename does not match the primary export. This looks like a copy-paste slip from the exporter params file, and it collides conceptually with the separate `UpdateCampfireExporterParams.ts`. It also means `Parameters.UpdateCampfireExportDateParams` will not resolve to this file's declared name.

Rename the type to match the file:

```ts
type UpdateCampfireExportDateParams = {
    policyID: string;
    value: CampfireExportDate;
};

export default UpdateCampfireExportDateParams;
```

This file is named `HeaderTitle.tsx` but its primary/default export is a function named `Header`. Per `DIRECTORIES.md`, a non-platform-specific file must be named after what it exports, so the basename and export name should match. Rename the component to match the file:

```tsx
function HeaderTitle({children, style, dialogLabel = '', shouldSkipFocusAfterTransition = false}: HeaderProps) {
    // ...
}

export default HeaderTitle;
```

This new file is named `index.ts` but it is not a platform-variant barrel — it contains a single real implementation (`getMergeSetupLink`) as its only (default) export. Per the file-naming philosophy, non-platform files must be named after what they export, never `index.ts`, so the module is discoverable and searchable by its export name. The sibling files in this same new `merge/` folder already follow this (`HR.ts`, `ATS.ts`), and the pre-existing `connections/index.ts` is an established multi-export directory entry point, which does not apply here.

Rename the file to match its export and update the import at `src/pages/workspace/hr/utils.ts`:

```ts
// src/libs/actions/connections/merge/getMergeSetupLink.ts
function getMergeSetupLink(policyID: string, integration: MergeHRProviderSlug | MergeATSProviderSlug) { ... }
export default getMergeSetupLink;
```

```ts
// caller
import getMergeSetupLink from '@libs/actions/connections/merge/getMergeSetupLink';
```

The directory was renamed from `ReceiptView` to `DynamicReceiptView`, but the component and its default export are still named `ReceiptView`. Per `DIRECTORIES.md`, a module should be named after (and discoverable by) its export. The effective module name here is the directory `DynamicReceiptView`, yet it default-exports `ReceiptView`, so the two diverge. This is also inconsistent with the sibling migrated in this same PR (`DynamicHoldReasonPage`, which renamed both the file and the component) and with the established `Dynamic*` convention (e.g. `DynamicIOURequestStepReport`).

Rename the component and its export to match the directory:

```tsx
function DynamicReceiptView({route}: DynamicReceiptViewProps) {
    // ...
}

export default DynamicReceiptView;
```

Per `DIRECTORIES.md`, a file must be named after what it exports. This file is `DualEntryExpensifyCardAccountPage.tsx`, but its component (and default export) is named `DualEntryCompanyCardAccountPage` — a copy-paste from the sibling company-card page. The `displayName` string below is likewise `"DualEntryCompanyCardAccountPage"`. This makes the module undiscoverable by its filename and misreports its identity.

Rename the component and its `displayName` to match the file:

```tsx
function DualEntryExpensifyCardAccountPage({policy}: WithPolicyConnectionsProps) {
    // ...
    // displayName="DualEntryExpensifyCardAccountPage"
}

export default withPolicyConnections(DualEntryExpensifyCardAccountPage);
```

This new file is named `RilletCardAccountSelector.tsx`, but its component and default export are named `RilletCompanyCardAccountPage`. Per DIRECTORIES.md, a file must be named after what it exports so the module is discoverable by its export name. The mismatch also hides a functional bug: the screen `RILLET_CARD_ACCOUNT_SELECTOR` (per-card account selection) is wired to this file, yet the component renders the company-card-account selector and calls `updateRilletCreditCardAccount` on the workspace-level `creditCardAccountCode` instead of the per-card account.

Rename/rewrite the component to match the file's intent, e.g.:

```tsx
function RilletCardAccountSelector({policy, route: {params: {feed, cardID}}}: RilletCardAccountSelectorProps) {
    // ...select and persist the per-card export account (NVP_RILLET_EXPORT_ACCOUNT) for this cardID...
}

export default withPolicyConnections(RilletCardAccountSelector);
```
